075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x,y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411635} {:answer/id #uuid "6bedfcc9-437c-5968-9d1f-c9181149a25b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411645} {:answer/id #uuid "bdfdb3f3-eb7e-53f5-a92b-07ae1f9ba9c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411657} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f350af-a521-541c-8fbf-4f974871b836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bedfcc9-437c-5968-9d1f-c9181149a25b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411645} :answer-field/id #uuid "a96a19a5-4d57-5c70-b981-f077301f5ca5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e3a6733-3b0e-5ea6-a342-f677785c09f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-212308" :question/problem "Let $A=R,$ and consider the relation \"greater than or equal to\" on $A.$ Express the relation using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\nR=\\{{{field-1}}\\in {{field-2}}\\,:\\,{{field-3}}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nFor a relation $R$ on a set $A,$ we also have the following notations:\n\n- $a\\,R\\,b$ if and only if $(a,b)\\in R$\n\n- $a\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,b$ if and only if $(a,b)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}$ such that $x\\ge y.$\n\nTherefore, we can write out $R$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge y\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff71f3f5-8383-5815-ba42-289f14e16b57" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordered Pairs Belonging to a Standard Relation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f7679f-73f5-5a0e-a03f-dee0f63f3f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411622} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddca7f1-da8d-5b3b-a8af-f028a712c9a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411629} {:answer/id #uuid "3e90ef8b-8d7b-59de-9f36-95572e6e5a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411633} {:answer/id #uuid "47f66e07-d1c0-5f79-82e3-fcb796015fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411636} {:answer/id #uuid "73e34a12-d804-5c4c-b0b0-abf70c77b752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08f7679f-73f5-5a0e-a03f-dee0f63f3f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411622} :answer-field/id #uuid "2932e32f-ef14-5573-93fb-85f8c4797761" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "579647d8-78b0-511b-8a94-882ec6fc1b22" :question/math-academy-id "q-212923" :question/problem "Given the relation $R$ on $N$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x>y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $6\\,R\\,8$\n\nII. $7\\,R\\,2$\n\nIII. $9\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,1$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x>y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $>$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $≯$ (or, equivalently, $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $\\le $) are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $>,$ we have $6>8,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $>,$ we have $7>2,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $\\le ,$ we have $9\\le 1,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff71f3f5-8383-5815-ba42-289f14e16b57" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordered Pairs Belonging to a Standard Relation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f17869c-0ece-5ef7-a77d-d4d3abc9fe0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411626} {:answer/id #uuid "4de08473-7aaa-5afc-bd97-107dd067e857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411638} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb80eb9-1870-59ae-9a1d-0cda06ea7804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411659} {:answer/id #uuid "d24a8b3d-e9ae-577a-92d3-05c2bbc465f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411660} {:answer/id #uuid "f4fc5035-39d1-5b29-9501-8255ecdfee6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4fc5035-39d1-5b29-9501-8255ecdfee6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411666} :answer-field/id #uuid "48ac91af-23e6-5125-89c4-136992dc2060" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8c98d98-74bc-5116-9f06-75c38fdcf981" :question/math-academy-id "q-212926" :question/problem "Given the relation $R$ on $Z$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x∣y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $6\\,R\\,3$\n\nII. $5\\,R\\,8$\n\nIII. $7\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,9$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $∣$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $∤$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $6∣3,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $5∣8,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $∤,$ we have $7∤9,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d05194c-249d-54fb-91f1-f614c0f5e584" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordered Pairs Belonging to a Non-Standard Relation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52d27a60-60f5-5754-a7bb-f26a821748ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411640} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8dfe07-93a1-57b4-9823-f9fcd6b4b485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411643} {:answer/id #uuid "665cfac7-9e55-5543-a679-b699d85f4d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411644} {:answer/id #uuid "9f73524d-1b4f-5845-812e-02bb16d1c56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411651} {:answer/id #uuid "eb20f44b-f74b-58f6-88d3-202204570fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f73524d-1b4f-5845-812e-02bb16d1c56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411651} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d5bc513-fdb3-5851-90bf-ada4837ec06e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5b2a52b-fd77-5422-b1dc-3a9ca690060e" :question/math-academy-id "q-212983" :question/problem "Given the relation $R$ on $Z$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x≡y+4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $0\\,R\\,2$\n\nII. $1\\,R\\,9$\n\nIII. $4\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,13$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y+4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ or equivalently, $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $6∣(x-y-4).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $6∣(0-2-4),$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $6∣(1-9-4),$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, we have $6∤(4-13-4),$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3fdfc79-dbfa-5820-82fb-402014341a3d" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Relations Using Set-Builder Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e6adea3-006c-533e-a33c-0dfacf799aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411623} {:answer/id #uuid "1acafbe5-6096-5090-b29d-42eefc9ae48b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411624} {:answer/id #uuid "741ee00f-ad3e-5619-965b-649f11ff695b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411647} {:answer/id #uuid "9bab72df-862a-5709-8953-a8ede8ef4eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "741ee00f-ad3e-5619-965b-649f11ff695b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411647} :answer-field/id #uuid "40a0cbce-0759-532f-9ab4-aa4e34ae8973" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411669} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "316794dc-dda4-5deb-8016-1610db41a730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411630} {:answer/id #uuid "322a920c-999f-5ebf-b81b-f1fdff28d7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3|(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411632} {:answer/id #uuid "55b6da04-b7f1-56ee-be5c-efe8f3b01e06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411641} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e5a5ab-050e-50ac-994f-b0673a0c7aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3|y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "322a920c-999f-5ebf-b81b-f1fdff28d7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3|(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411632} :answer-field/id #uuid "70efa87b-beda-518f-8263-32706eed461b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411671} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04b7cd61-6d77-5fba-b3b8-52a35ad4aff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411621} {:answer/id #uuid "31da6476-a086-5047-92af-0b7f80a9e936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x,x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411631} {:answer/id #uuid "3f926afe-f465-5842-9bd3-aad8b7d5dc0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x,y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411634} {:answer/id #uuid "569f3998-444b-57f3-96fb-62c9f4fff70d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411642} {:answer/id #uuid "ac1b4092-e403-5303-8926-e49996d57fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f926afe-f465-5842-9bd3-aad8b7d5dc0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x,y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411634} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbaddc60-4ab1-53ea-a39d-217315412f69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2674a53-8856-5a0e-8ea3-7bef241518ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-218358" :question/problem "Let $A=Z,$ and consider the relation $R$ on $A$ such that $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $3\\,|\\,(x+y).$ Express $R$ using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\nR=\\{{{field-1}}\\in {{field-2}}\\,:\\,{{field-3}}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nFor a relation $R$ on a set $A,$ we also have the following notations:\n\n- $a\\,R\\,b$ if and only if $(a,b)\\in R$\n\n- $a\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,b$ if and only if $(a,b)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}$ such that $3\\,|\\,(x+y).$\n\nTherefore, we can write out $R$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\,|\\,(x+y)\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d05194c-249d-54fb-91f1-f614c0f5e584" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordered Pairs Belonging to a Non-Standard Relation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ec285a-89b6-5e80-85cf-547592c0be93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411627} {:answer/id #uuid "29192154-5fca-5c4c-ba05-c1677c12f786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411628} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6224af-2752-5783-be20-5ad5862f62d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411637} {:answer/id #uuid "516f8b78-7d59-5ec9-ba7c-04b73f06ad86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411639} {:answer/id #uuid "85e2f0d3-5246-5ae6-9a99-5800a2b254f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24ec285a-89b6-5e80-85cf-547592c0be93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411627} :answer-field/id #uuid "856a64a3-53df-597c-9cc5-75ee45f124c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e6ad6a4-a09f-5fba-92c0-ff271a2f98be" :question/math-academy-id "q-80860" :question/problem "Given the relation $R$ on $Z$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x∣(y-1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $4\\,R\\,13$\n\nII. $3\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,11$\n\nIII. $8\\,R\\,3$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣(y-1).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $4∣(13-1),$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $3∤(11-1),$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $8∣(3-1),$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
